2886b8abda1cfeb5f20101","file":"preprints/The-weak-pinned-planar-distance-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","line":39,"producer_hash_method":"atlas-whitespace-normalized-text","producer_statement_sha256":"d279168a717b19349237cc8b41cb5fba83ef41aad7e087eb38da56e9e6cb3119","provenance":{"kind":"computed","pin":"fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb","source":"preprints/The-weak-pinned-planar-distance-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex"},"sha256":"sha256:771f480d2eb258d0bd21432bf5a6ddc19ab032fd392886b8abda1cfeb5f20101","url":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-weak-pinned-planar-distance-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex#L39"},"statement_kind":"corollary","text":"\\label{cor:pins}\nFor every fixed $\\varepsilon>0$,\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\substack{P\\subset\\R^2\\\\|P|=n}}\n \\frac{\\bigl|\\{x\\in P:|D_x(P)|<n^{1-\\varepsilon}\\}\\bigr|}{n}\n \\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nIn particular, every sufficiently large $n$-point planar set has a pin\ndetermining at least $n^{1-\\varepsilon}$ distinct nonzero distances."}
